@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: sbx capabilities proveo re-implements, and the one belief now falsified
' covers: internal/sbx/kit.go
' covers: internal/sbx/agents.go
' The contract this corrects: _spec/internal/sbx/sbx-kit-contract.puml
' The rebase that started it: _spec/_devops/sandbox-template-rebase.puml
' Notes here are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── AUTHORITATIVE SOURCES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   SPEC-v2   : https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib/blob/main/spec/SPEC-v2.md
'   kits      : https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kits/
'   examples  : https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kit-examples/
'   pi kit    : https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib/tree/main/pi
'   contrib   : https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib
'   sbx ssh   : https://www.ajeetraina.com/ssh-straight-into-your-agent-sandboxes-a-hands-on-look-at-sbx-ssh/
'
' ── FALSIFIED: "THE AGENT LIST IS CLOSED" ───────────────────────────────────
' sbx-kit-contract.puml states, under AGENT IDENTITY: "`sbx run --help` prints a
' CLOSED list ... A Kit cannot add to it ... An invented agent therefore never
' resolves agents/<name>/spec.yml, its binding gate is skipped, and the
' interactive session drops within seconds." That is true of `kind: mixin`, and
' it is the reason proveo contributes a mixin beside a built-in agent.
'
' It is NOT true of `kind: sandbox`. SPEC-v2: "A sandbox kit is a COMPLETE AGENT.
' It MUST declare a `sandbox:` block and MAY declare every shared block." Its
' `name` is the identifier `sbx run <name> --kit <path>` selects. The refusal we
' met — "built-in agents cannot be overridden by a kit" — is about SHADOWING an
' existing name, which is a different operation from adding a new one.
'
' Two witnesses that this is the intended path, not a loophole:
'   pi       a community kit for an agent sbx never shipped, with its own image
'            (docker.io/sbx/pi-image), its own entrypoint, and credentials the
'            sandbox proxy injects per request.
'   copilot  WAS a built-in; the kit approach now "replaces that functionality
'            for non-built-in agents".
'
' ── WHAT A SANDBOX KIT CAN DECLARE THAT proveo BUILDS BY HAND ───────────────
'   sandbox.image        the image the sandbox boots from
'   sandbox.entrypoint   fixed process prefix; [0] is the binary
'   sandbox.command      {default, interactive} — the mode-specific tail
'   credentials[].apiKey  inject[].domain + header, host-side proxy, per request
'                         (domain MUST also appear in permissions.network.allow)
'   volumes[]            block or tmpfs, path + size
'   ports[]              inbound exposure
'   agentInstructions    filename + content — the AI profile file
'   setup.files[]        path + content + mode, written at startup
'
' ── CANDIDATE EXPERIMENTS, RANKED BY WHAT THEY RETIRE ───────────────────────
' E1  cecli as a first-class agent (`kind: sandbox`)   -- BUILT, HOST-BLOCKED
'     Retires the shell-agent launch entirely — the `-c` wrapper, bash -l
'     semantics, and the whole class of defect that made `-- cecli` run
'     `bash cecli`. cecli declares its own entrypoint the way pi does.
'
'     SHIPPED BEHIND A GATE: PROVEO_SBX_AGENT_KIT. Off, a def with no built-in
'     agent borrows `shell` with the flag-leading launch, which is the path that
'     WAS measured and negative-checked. On, sandbox.Spec renders
'     `kind: sandbox`, names the agent proveo-<target>, and passes extras as
'     bare words — there is no bash in between to reinterpret them.
'
'     The Kit names the IMAGE and stops there. The def already declares
'     ENTRYPOINT ["dumb-init","--","<target>-entrypoint"] and CMD ["<target>"],
'     and that pair is what the docker backend runs and what works there.
'     Restating it in the Kit would be a second copy free to drift from the
'     first. If sbx turns out to need the prefix declared, it goes in then.
'
'     Only the IDENTITY changes: permissions, environment and setup are carried
'     across byte-for-byte, asserted as a diff against the mixin built from the
'     same Input rather than against fixed values — the first version of that
'     test asserted a non-empty allowlist and was measuring its own fixture.
'
'     HOW TO MEASURE IT. The gate rearranges the ladder instead of adding to it:
'     with an agent of its own there is no borrowed command, so the 5-rung
'     shell-agent ladder collapses back to 4 and the launch arrives with the Kit
'     at rung 3. Only a rung CARRYING the Kit may name our agent, because the
'     Kit is what declares the name.
'
'         PROVEO_LADDER_TEST=1 PROVEO_LADDER_TARGET=cecli \
'         PROVEO_SBX_AGENT_KIT=1 go test -tags e2e ./e2e/ -run Ladder -v
'
'     Green on rung 3 means the borrowed shell can go and sbx.ShellLaunch with
'     it. Red means the docs promise something sbx does not do for a kit built
'     this way, and the gate stays off — which is the whole reason it is a gate.
'
'     CANNOT BE CLIMBED FROM INSIDE A SANDBOX, measured 2026-09-06. Two
'     independent blocks, and neither is a policy against recursion:
'       the sbx CLI is not installed in any proveo image — nothing in defs/
'         fetches it, and it is a HOST tool that creates microVMs
'       /dev/kvm does not exist in the sandbox and no kvm module is loaded
'     Docker's install requirements: "If you're running inside a virtual machine
'     or virtual desktop infrastructure environment, the environment must support
'     NESTED VIRTUALIZATION." sbx nests where the platform exposes KVM; this
'     sandbox does not expose it, so the ladder is a host-side measurement.
'     Everything below the sbx boundary IS verified here: `mise run build all`
'     (14 images) and `mise run test-images` (351 passed, 0 failed) both green.
'
' E2  sbx-native credential injection (credentials[].apiKey)  -- RESCOPED
'
'     THE ENTRY BELOW WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, and the correction makes it
'     a smaller and better experiment. It read: "Overlaps internal/broker and
'     internal/egressproxy on the sbx backend ... Retires the most proveo-specific
'     code of any item here. Risk: high — the DLP scanner and off-route stripping
'     have no declared equivalent."
'
'     Measured in the tree: internal/backend/sandbox imports NEITHER broker NOR
'     egresspolicy NOR egressproxy. The broker runs on the docker+egress backend
'     only. So on sbx nothing is retired — and nothing is lost either, because
'     the DLP scan does not run there to begin with.
'
'     What IS true is smaller and worth fixing. sandbox.Spec's addSecret reads
'     `v := in.Lookup(name)` — the REAL key — and hands it to `sbx secret set`,
'     so on the sbx backend THE AGENT PROCESS HOLDS THE REAL CREDENTIAL. proveo's
'     own sentinel (entrypoint.DefaultSentinel, "proveo-brokered") is applied in
'     internal/run for the brokered path, not here.
'
'     sbx offers exactly the missing half: `credentials[].apiKey.proxyManaged:
'     true` sets the named env var to a SENTINEL and injects the real value
'     host-side, per request, only into `inject[].domain` with `header`/`format`
'     — and that domain MUST also appear in permissions.network.allow.
'
'     THE OFF-ROUTE STRIP IS SUBSUMED, not lost. broker.Apply deletes
'     authorization/x-api-key/x-goog-api-key/api-key/proxy-authorization from any
'     request whose host is off-route. On sbx the baseline is deny-all or
'     balanced (_spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml), so an unallowlisted
'     host is BLOCKED outright rather than merely stripped — a strictly stronger
'     outcome for the same threat.
'
'     So E2 is a strict gain for the sbx path, at the cost of one unknown.
'
'     MEASURED 2026-09-06, AND IT DECIDES THE SHAPE. A probe mixin declaring
'     credentials for service "anthropic" was refused by sbx before the sandbox
'     was created:
'
'       400 Bad Request: kit_artifacts: compose: credential for service
'       "anthropic" defined in both "shell" and "credprobe"
'
'     The built-in agent ALREADY DECLARES that credential. sbx-kit-contract.puml
'     recorded this rule from the vendor docs — "a mixin repeating a service the
'     agent already declares is rejected outright" — and the probe is the first
'     time it has been met head-on rather than read.
'
'     So E2 IS NOT AVAILABLE TO A MIXIN, which is what proveo contributes for
'     every def backed by a built-in agent (claudecode -> claude, cursor,
'     opencode). Those three cannot declare credentials[] at all, at any risk
'     level, because the parent already did.
'
'     E2 IS ONLY REACHABLE ON THE E1 PATH. A `kind: sandbox` kit is a complete
'     agent with no parent to collide with, so it MAY declare every shared block
'     — credentials included. cecli is the one def in that position, and only
'     because E1 put it there. The two experiments are one feature.
'
'     For the other three the equivalent would mean abandoning the vendor's
'     built-in agent for a proveo- prefixed one (a built-in name cannot be
'     shadowed), trading the agent wiring sbx maintains for a credential
'     property sbx may already provide. Not obviously worth it, and not
'     measured — see the open question below.
'
'     ANSWERED, and it inverts the experiment. On the stock shell agent:
'         sbx run shell . -- -c 'echo "VALUE: ${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-<unset>}"'
'         VALUE: proxy-managed
'
'     sbx ALREADY DOES E2 for every def backed by a built-in agent. The agent's
'     env holds the sentinel, not the key; the host-side proxy injects. There is
'     nothing to build for claudecode, cursor or opencode — which is fortunate,
'     because the "defined in both" refusal above means we could not have built
'     it for them anyway. The two facts are the same fact seen from either side:
'     the credential is the AGENT's to declare, and the built-ins declare it.
'
'     (Note the probe command: the first version used a BARE first word after
'     `--` and sbx ran `bash '<the whole string>'`, which failed with "No such
'     file or directory" — the identical defect as `-- cecli`, in a command
'     written to test something else. shell-agent-launch.puml draws this exact
'     case. `-c` leads with a flag and is appended instead.)
'
'     ── AND IT EXPOSES A REGRESSION INSIDE E1 ──────────────────────────────
'     Follow the two measurements together:
'       gate OFF  cecli borrows `shell`, `shell` declares the credential,
'                 so cecli's env holds a SENTINEL
'       gate ON   cecli runs agent proveo-cecli from OUR sandbox kit, and
'                 sbx.Kit has no Credentials field at all — nothing declares
'                 the credential, so nothing proxy-manages it
'
'     E1 therefore risks moving cecli from sentinel to REAL KEY in the agent
'     process: a credential downgrade hidden inside a launch fix. It is exactly
'     the class of defect the gate exists to catch, and it is why the gate must
'     not be defaulted on until sbx.Kit can declare credentials[] and the
'     cecli kit does.
'
'     So E2's remaining work is not an enhancement for three defs. It is a
'     PREREQUISITE for one: the def that declares its own agent must also
'     declare its own credentials, under the SERVICE name the bindings use, or
'     it silently loses the property every other def gets for free.
'
'     ── BUILT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
'     sbx.Kit now carries Credentials, and the sandbox-kit branch of
'     sandbox.Spec fills it from proveo's OWN provider registry — the same
'     source the broker uses, so the two cannot drift. Both answer one question:
'     which key attaches to which host under which header.
'
'     WHAT IS SKIPPED, AND WHY. A provider is left out when it is not
'     broker-injectable at all (signed requests: bedrock, azure, vertex —
'     nothing to attach), when no key is present, or when it authenticates by
'     QUERY PARAMETER. sbx injects into a header and has no query form, so
'     declaring such a service would sentinel the variable and then attach the
'     value nowhere — strictly worse than leaving it alone.
'
'     THE SECRET IS STORED TWICE, on purpose. sbx resolves credentials[].service
'     against a secret under the SERVICE name; proveo has always stored under
'     the ENV VAR name, and `sbx secret ls` showed both shapes coexisting. The
'     env-var entry stays because the gate-off path still relies on it.
'
'     provider.Resolved gained a Bearer field. The Kit declares injection as a
'     FORMAT STRING ("Bearer %s" vs "%s") and never sees the value, so the
'     scheme has to survive on its own rather than be sniffed back out of a
'     prefixed Value.
'
'     ── WHAT STILL BLOCKS DEFAULTING THE GATE ON ───────────────────────────
'     One measurement, and it is cheap. Everything above is verified in Go —
'     ten tests, five negative checks that all fire — but nothing has been run
'     against a real sbx, and credentials are the subsystem where a wrong answer
'     is not a degraded run but NO run.
'
'     Two specific unknowns the render exposes:
'       DOMAIN FORM. proveo's registry stores hosts as suffix patterns
'         (".anthropic.com"), and that is what the Kit now emits. sbx's own
'         bindings file uses both ".anthropic.com" and "api.anthropic.com", but
'         the kit EXAMPLE in the docs uses the bare host. If sbx rejects the
'         dotted form in inject[].domain it fails at create time with a 400.
'       SERVICE SECRET. Whether `sbx secret set anthropic <api key>` is accepted
'         for a service whose stored credential is currently an OAuth login.
'
'     Both fall out of one command:
'         PROVEO_SBX_AGENT_KIT=1 PROVEO_LADDER_TEST=1 \
'         PROVEO_LADDER_TARGET=cecli go test -tags e2e ./e2e/ -run Ladder -v
'     Rung 3 carries the Kit. Green means the declaration parses, the domains
'     are accepted and the agent still reaches a prompt; a 400 names which of
'     the two guesses was wrong.
'
'     ── RUN 2026-09-07: E1 CONFIRMED, CREDENTIALS UNTOUCHED ────────────────
'     Every rung held, and the shape assertion printed what it measured:
'         kit shape measured: kind: sandbox, name: proveo-cecli
'     So the sandbox kit registers an agent, sbx resolves proveo-cecli, and the
'     session reaches a prompt and holds 45s. E1 is measured, not inferred.
'
'     The credentials block was NOT exercised, and the ladder CANNOT exercise
'     it. childEnvArgsNoCredential unsets every provider.DetectVars() entry so
'     a climb never spends a real key — by design, and correct. With nothing
'     detected, provider.Detect returns nothing and the kit renders with ZERO
'     credentials. Confirmed by rendering under the ladder's own environment:
'     `credentials in kit: 0, secrets: 0`.
'
'     Reading that PASS as "credentials work" would be the sixth green result
'     this session that measured its own setup. It measured E1, which is real,
'     and nothing else.
'
'     WHAT DOES EXERCISE IT: a standalone `kind: sandbox` kit carrying the
'     block verbatim with a DUMMY key. proxyManaged means the agent never sees
'     the value, so a dummy proves everything that matters — that the block
'     parses, that a suffix-pattern domain is accepted, and that the variable
'     arrives as a sentinel — without spending a real credential.
'
'     ── PROBED 2026-09-07: BOTH GUESSES GOOD ───────────────────────────────
'         sbx run credprobe-own . --kit /tmp/cp3
'         ✓ Created sandbox credprobe-own-sandbox
'         VALUE: proxy-managed
'
'     A `kind: sandbox` kit declaring credentials[] is ACCEPTED — no "defined in
'     both", no 400 — which is the other half of the mixin refusal: the
'     credential belongs to whoever declares the agent, and this kit does.
'
'     The SUFFIX-PATTERN domain is accepted. proveo's registry stores hosts as
'     ".anthropic.com" and that is what the Kit emits; the docs example uses a
'     bare host, so this was a guess. It is not one now, and the builder needs
'     no change.
'
'     And the variable arrives as a SENTINEL. That closes the downgrade E1
'     opened: a def declaring its own agent now keeps the property it would
'     otherwise have silently lost.
'
'     STILL UNPROVEN, and deliberately so: whether the proxy can INJECT a
'     working value for a service whose stored credential is an OAuth login.
'     The sentinel appears either way — it follows from the declaration, not
'     from the secret resolving — so only a real API call settles it. The
'     downgrade risk is closed regardless, because a sentinel that fails to
'     authenticate is a visible error, and a real key in the agent process is
'     not.
'
'     ── ASSERTED IN THE SUITE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
'     e2e TestSandboxKitProxyManagesTheCredential does what the ladder cannot:
'     it puts ONE credential back — a dummy — renders proveo's real Kit, starts
'     the sandbox, and asks the LIVE sandbox what the agent actually holds. It
'     fails loudly on the dummy (the downgrade), on <unset> (a declaration
'     resolving against nothing), and on a Kit that renders no credentials at
'     all with a key present.
'
'     It rests on one property of env(1): options are parsed before assignments,
'     so `env -u X X=v` SETS X, while the reverse order makes env treat -u as a
'     filename and fail. Two unit tests pin that, because it is invisible at the
'     call site and fatal if inverted.
'
'     ── THE FIRST VERSION OF THAT TEST PASSED FALSELY ──────────────────────
'     Worth keeping, because it is the sixth in this session and the first one
'     written to PREVENT exactly this class:
'
'         ✅ credential is proxy-managed: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY holds
'         "ERROR: no sandbox named 'proveo-credprobe-cecli-49376' ..."
'
'     Two faults compounding. It read the variable with `sbx exec` AFTER the
'     session ended, by which time sbx had removed the sandbox — so the probe
'     could not run. And probeCredential returned sbx's ERROR TEXT as if it were
'     a value, which the switch accepted because it named three failure cases
'     and treated everything else as success.
'
'     The rule that came out of it: A PROBE THAT CANNOT RUN MUST FAIL, NEVER
'     PASS. An assertion whose default branch is success will eventually be fed
'     something that is not a measurement, and it will say yes.
'
'     Rewritten to read the variable IN THE AGENT PROCESS — the only place it
'     means anything — by swapping the Kit's entrypoint and reading the session
'     output. The credentials block under test is still the one proveo renders;
'     only the entrypoint changes, so it cannot drift from a hand-copy.
'
'     Two parsing details the rewrite forced, both now pinned by table tests:
'       session output reaches the test through plain(), which collapses EVERY
'         run of whitespace including newlines, so there are no lines to parse —
'         which is why renderPostureKit splits on spaces
'       so the value is DELIMITED, PROVEO_CRED_VALUE[...], because an
'         unterminated marker would swallow whatever printed next and an empty
'         value would be indistinguishable from a missing one
'
'     ── AND THE CORRECTED TEST FOUND SOMETHING REAL ────────────────────────
'     Its first honest failure was worth more than the false pass:
'
'         This kit wants to use these credentials:
'           opencode     API key → sent to .opencode.ai   (not set)
'         [A]pprove all · [R]eview each · [N]o:
'
'     DECLARING A CREDENTIAL sbx HOLDS NO SECRET FOR MAKES IT ASK. The run stops
'     on a human, which for an unattended agent is a hang. This is the same
'     class as the blockedMarkers idle_test.go already tracks — alive, but
'     unable to proceed — and it is the last real obstacle to defaulting the
'     gate on.
'
'     WHY THAT KIT DECLARED opencode WITH NOTHING STORED: the credential is
'     declared from proveo's lookup, which found a key on the host, but the
'     SECRET is stored by proveo's Run path — and the test renders the Kit with
'     `--print` and then calls `sbx run` itself, bypassing it. So the Kit named
'     a service sbx had nothing under.
'
'     Which means the open question is narrower than it looks: in a REAL proveo
'     run the service-named secret is stored before `sbx run` (Spec returns the
'     secrets, Run sets them, then builds argv), so the prompt may not appear at
'     all. Whether storing a secret also counts as approval is not known, and it
'     is exactly what decides whether the gate can default on.
'
'     THE TEST MUST NOT SETTLE IT BY WRITING. `sbx secret set` is HOST-WIDE and
'     outlives the run — proveo warns about this itself — so a test that stored
'     a dummy would overwrite the operator's real credential. It now reads
'     `sbx secret ls` instead and declares only services sbx already knows,
'     skipping with a reason when there are none.
'
'     ── ONE MORE THING THE RUN SURFACED ────────────────────────────────────
'         ⚠ template "proveo/cecli:local" was built for the "shell" agent but
'           you are using "proveo-cecli".
'     sbx associates a loaded template with the AGENT it was loaded for, and
'     freshTemplate loads ours for `shell`. Non-fatal today — every rung still
'     held — but it means the image store is keyed by an agent name that E1
'     changes, and that is worth settling before the gate defaults on.
'
'     THE BINDINGS FILE EXISTS AND IS POPULATED. ~/.config/sbx/credentials.yaml
'     carries bindings for anthropic, cursor, google, openai and xai, each a
'     list of apiKey.domains. `sbx secret ls` shows BOTH naming schemes side by
'     side: `anthropic (oauth configured)` — sbx's own service, the one bindings
'     reference — and `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (stored)`, which is proveo's, named
'     after the env var. A kit's credentials[].service resolves against the
'     former, so any E2 work must set secrets under the SERVICE name.
'
'     Gated when built, like E1: credentials are the one subsystem where a wrong
'     answer is not a degraded run but no run at all.
'
' E3  `sbx ssh` as the e2e entry path
'     sbx-kit-contract records that the shell agent "does NOT survive a
'     detached tmux pane", so e2e cannot drive an sbx session to a prompt and
'     e2e/sbx_test.go skips. `sbx setup ssh` makes `ssh <name>.sbx` reach the
'     sandbox through the daemon socket. Retires a SKIP, not code — which is
'     why it may be the cheapest real win here.
'
' E4  per-agent `-docker` templates instead of shell-docker
'     claudecode/cursor/opencode could extend claude-code-docker,
'     cursor-agent-docker, opencode-docker and inherit the vendor's own agent
'     wiring. Conflicts with _spec/_devops/agent-version-pin.puml, which pins
'     and asserts exact versions; a template's preinstalled agent moves on the
'     template's schedule. cecli has no variant, so shell-docker stays anyway.
'
' E5  branch mode / per-run worktrees
'     sbx gives each run its own worktree under .sbx/. proveo has clone mode
'     (_spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml). Likely overlapping, and the
'     comparison has not been made.
'
' E6  agentInstructions + setup.files vs proveo-seed
'     House rules and settings are composed by proveo-seed today. Some of that
'     is declarable. The seed also runs on the docker backend, so this splits
'     one mechanism into two — recorded as a question, not a proposal.
'
' NONE OF THESE IS MEASURED. Every line above is read from docs and one
' community kit; nothing here has been run. The last time a plan in this tree
' rested on a plausible reading of sbx's behaviour it cost 210 MB per image and
' four manifests promising a daemon no image carried.
' ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

title Experiment — sbx Kit capabilities proveo re-implements

component "SPEC-v2\nkind: sandbox is a COMPLETE AGENT" as SPEC <<cloud>>
component "pi / copilot kits\nnon-built-in agents, shipped" as WIT <<cloud>>

node "proveo today" as NOW <<host>> {
  component "shell agent + -c wrapper\ncecli has no sbx agent" as SHELL <<error>>
  component "internal/broker\ninject on-route · strip off-route" as BROKER <<app>>
  component "proveo-seed\nhouse rules · settings · trust" as SEED <<app>>
  component "e2e/sbx_test.go\nskips: no tmux-survivable session" as SKIP <<error>>
}

component "E1 cecli as kind: sandbox" as E1 <<db>>
component "E2 credentials[].apiKey.inject" as E2 <<db>>
component "E3 sbx ssh as the e2e entry" as E3 <<db>>

SPEC ARROW_MAIN E1 : a name sbx run selects
WIT ARROW_MAIN E1 : two working precedents
SHELL ARROW_QUEUE E1 : what it would retire
BROKER ARROW_QUEUE E2 : overlaps, does not equal
SKIP ARROW_QUEUE E3 : retires a skip, not code
SEED ARROW_QUEUE E2 : declarable in part

note bottom of E1
  **Closed to mixins, not to kits.** A sandbox kit
  declares a complete agent and names it, so the
  harness sbx has no agent for can stop borrowing
  the shell's — and stop inheriting the shell's
  argument semantics along with it.
end note

note bottom of E2
  **Overlap is not equivalence.** sbx injects a
  key per domain; the broker also STRIPS off-route
  and runs a DLP scan on the way out. Taking the
  first half while assuming the second rides along
  loses the half nobody declared.
end note

note bottom of E3
  **The cheapest item retires a skip.** A suite
  that skips reports success by testing nothing,
  and this one skips precisely where sbx sessions
  live — so the gap sits under the backend proveo
  actually ships on.
end note

note bottom of SHELL
  **A borrowed agent brings its contract.** The
  shell agent's rule for words after `--` is not
  cecli's rule, and a def that borrows the launch
  inherits every semantic attached to it, seen or
  unseen.
end note

note bottom of SPEC
  **Read before adopting, measured before ship.**
  Every claim here comes from docs and one
  community kit; none has been run, and the last
  plan resting on a plausible reading cost 210 MB
  an image and four broken promises.
end note

@enduml
